From f60e928d3b8ada13893bb315c20549c7989131d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 12:44:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Handshake: a real clasp, the hands crossed and wrapped around
 each other

The two hands of the handshake passed through each other. Both robots
put the middle of the palm on the same point with the hands nearly end
to end (9 degrees apart), so each closed hand wrapped the partner's
wrist cuff and forearm (up to 24 mm inside them), the thumbs crossed in
an X above the hands, the fingers closed while the hands still slid in
and opened while they slid out.

The clasp is now computed from the geometry when it is planned:

- Both robots put the web of the thumb on the same clasp point, the
  palm vertical and the fingers pitched 30 degrees down, so the hands
  cross at 60 degrees as in a real handshake. Each robot then knows the
  partner's hand without reading its motion: its own, turned half a
  turn about the vertical through the clasp point.
- The palm plane's azimuth and a lift of the elbow are chosen for a
  comfortable wrist at the clasp and through the pumps (a hand pointing
  down that far bent the wrist past its limit otherwise). The hands sit
  a little higher, clear of a king on a central square at the pumps'
  lowest.
- The fingers close onto a model of the partner's hand (the palm's
  section, its knuckles, the wrist cuff, the forearm, the thumb): the
  three joints close together and a phalanx that touches stops its joint
  and the ones before it, so they wrap the little-finger edge and rest
  on the back of the partner's hand. The thumb, turned over the
  partner's hand, rests on its back, clear of the partner's thumb.
- Phases: reach out open, slide the palms together, close the grip once
  they touch (closed just after the clasp event), two pumps, open the
  grip while the palms stay (release), slide apart, back to rest. The
  events, the duration and the pen laid down first keep their instants.
- A left-handed player's shaking hand (its writing hand) gets the same
  wrist relaxation as a right hand: coming straight from the pen, it
  flipped through its wrist limits. It stays the exact mirror image of a
  right-handed robot's hand through the clasp.

On the exact porcelain meshes, every 1/120 s of the whole handshake,
right- vs right-handed and right- vs left-handed: at most 0.1 mm of
contact (it was 24 mm). A new test checks the hands with capsules built
from the bones, which the old clasp fails.

